###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/Crack
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/Crack> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                          rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/Damage> ,
                                                                          [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                            owl:onProperty <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#generated> ;
                                                                            owl:someValuesFrom <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/PlasticZone>
                                                                          ] ,
                                                                          [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                            owl:onProperty <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/initiatesAt> ;
                                                                            owl:someValuesFrom <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/GrainBoundary>
                                                                          ] ,
                                                                          [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                            owl:onProperty <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/initiatesAt> ;
                                                                            owl:someValuesFrom <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/Inclusion>
                                                                          ] ,
                                                                          [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                            owl:onProperty <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/initiatesAt> ;
                                                                            owl:someValuesFrom <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/Intrusion>
                                                                          ] ,
                                                                          [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                            owl:onProperty <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/initiatesAt> ;
                                                                            owl:someValuesFrom <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/Surface>
                                                                          ] ;
                                                          rdfs:label "Crack"@en ;
                                                          <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Damage type which separates/cleaves a material. Depending of the crack scale, distinct driving forces are at play. "@en .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackArrestToughness
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackArrestToughness> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                         rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/MechanicalProperty> ;
                                                                         rdfs:label "Crack arrest toughness"@en ;
                                                                         <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The standard definition of crack arrest toughness relies on the static estimate KIa (ASTM E1221). Crack arrest toughness K_Ia is considered to represent the minimum fracture toughness of a material. If the fracture mechanical driving force K_I is less than K_Ia, fracture is not possible."@en .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackExtensionMode
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackExtensionMode> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                       rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/MechanicalProperty> ;
                                                                       rdfs:label "Crack extension mode"@en ;
                                                                       <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "refers to \"opening, shearing and tearing\" cracks"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackGrowthBehaviour
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackGrowthBehaviour> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                         rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/MicrostructuralProperty> ;
                                                                         rdfs:label "Crack growth behaviour"@en ;
                                                                         <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A specifier which describes the manner in which a crack propagates through a material under stress, e.g. \"brittle\", \"ductile\", or \"intergranular\"." ;
                                                                         <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/definitionSource> "GPT-4 generated description with manual adjustments" .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackInitation
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackInitation> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                   rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/Mechanism> ;
                                                                   rdfs:label "Crack initation"@en ;
                                                                   <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The crack initiation stage is reached after the crack nucleation and represents the stage where the crack extended beyond the (persistent) slip band, volume defect, the first microstructural barrier which caused its nucleation. At this stage, the mechanism of crack extension is less dependent on the local stress concentration, vacancy diffusion at slip bands, or phase boundaries but rather resembles modes II/III crack growth (shear-dominated)." ;
                                                                   <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/definitionSource> "GPT-4 generated description with manual adjustments" .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackLength
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackLength> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/MicrostructuralProperty> ;
                                                                rdfs:label "Crack length"@en ;
                                                                <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The instantaneous measurement of the distance between the crack origin and the crack tip." ;
                                                                <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/definitionSource> "GPT-4 generated description with manual adjustments" .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackNucleation
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackNucleation> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                    rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/Mechanism> ,
                                                                                    [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                                      owl:onProperty <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/precedes> ;
                                                                                      owl:someValuesFrom <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackInitation>
                                                                                    ] ;
                                                                    rdfs:label "Crack nucleation"@en ;
                                                                    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The nucleation of a crack in a material under stress caused by the interaction of lattice defects and local decohesion of atomic bonds." ;
                                                                    <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/definitionSource> "GPT-4 generated description with manual adjustments" .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackPropagation
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackPropagation> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                     rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/Mechanism> ;
                                                                     rdfs:label "Crack propagation"@en ;
                                                                     <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The extension of a crack in a material under stress." ;
                                                                     <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/definitionSource> "GPT-4 generated description with manual adjustments" .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackTipOpeningDisplacement
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackTipOpeningDisplacement> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                                rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/MicrostructuralProperty> ;
                                                                                rdfs:label "Crack tip opening displacement"@en ;
                                                                                <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A measure from fracture mechanics which expresses the distance between opposing faces of the crack at a defined position close to the crack tip during tensile loading and is typically applied for materials which show some degree of plastcity." ;
                                                                                <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/definitionSource> "GPT-4 generated description with manual adjustments" .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackinducedPlasticity
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CrackinducedPlasticity> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                           rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/Damage> ;
                                                                           rdfs:label "Crack-induced plasticity"@en ;
                                                                           <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The plastic deformation that occurs near the tip of a crack under stress." ;
                                                                           <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/definitionSource> "Description generated by GPT-4" .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CreepTest
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CreepTest> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                              rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/MechanicalTest> ;
                                                              rdfs:label "Creep test"@en ;
                                                              <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A mechanical test in which a static force is applied causing an elongation and the transition through different creep stages."@en .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CriticalPittingPotential
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CriticalPittingPotential> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                             rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CorrosionProperty> ;
                                                                             rdfs:label "Critical pitting potential"@en ;
                                                                             <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Critical pitting potential (E_pit) is the least positive potential at which pits can form. It is the potential at which the metal salt of the aggressive ion in solution is in equilibrium with the metal oxide."@en .


###  https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CriticalValueOfJIntegral
<https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/CriticalValueOfJIntegral> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                             rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/pmd/materials-mechanics-ontology/MechanicalProperty> ;
                                                                             rdfs:label "Critical value of j integral"@en ;
                                                                             <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The critical value of the J-integral which is a measure of the strain energy release rate above which large-scale plastic yielding during crack propagation is observed." ;
                                                                             <https://w3id.org/pmd/co/definitionSource> "GPT-4 generated description with manual adjustments" .


